Financial market data are not _deterministic_. In fact, all the variables under consideration, with the exception of the time variable \(t\), are likely to be _random_. Since time is continuous, we observe uncountably many random variables as time passes. Hence, \(F\left(S_{t},t\right),S_{t}\), and possibly the risk-free rate \(r_{t}\) are all _continuous-time stochastic processes_.

Can we then apply the same reasoning and use the same tools as in standard calculus to write

\[dF(t)=F_{s}dS_{t}+F_{r}dr_{t}+F_{t}\,dt \tag{4.18}\]

The answer to this question is no. It turns out that one needs a "new" calculus and a different formula when the variables under consideration are random processes. The following is a first look at some of these difficulties.

