#### Limiting Properties

An important element of the discussion involving the binomial process \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) is that the two possible values assumed by each \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) depend on the parameter \(\Delta\). This dependence permits a discussion of the _limiting behavior_ of the binomial process. We can ask a number of questions that will eventually relate to pricing derivative products.

One important question is the following: What does a typical path followed by the \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) look like? Clearly, such a trajectory will be made of a sequence of \(+a\sqrt{\Delta}\) and \(-a\sqrt{\Delta}\)'s. If the probability of each of these outcomes is exactly equal to \(1/2\), then a realization of \(\left\{\Delta F\left(t\right),t=t_{0},t_{0}+\Delta,\ldots\right\}\) will, as \(\Delta\) gets smaller, converge to an extremely erratic trajectory that fluctuates between \(+a\sqrt{\Delta}\) and \(-a\sqrt{\Delta}\).

In fact, as \(\Delta\) gets smaller, two things happen. First, the observation points come nearer, and second, \(\left|a\sqrt{\Delta}\right|\) gets smaller.

The \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) is the increment in the price process. What kind of a path is followed by \(F\left(t\right)\) itself? First, note that if \(F(t)\) represents the price of a derivative product at time \(t\), then it will equal the sum of all up- and downticks since \(t_{0}\). As \(\Delta\to 0,F(t)\) will be given by

\[F\left(t\right)=F\left(t_{0}\right)+\int_{t_{0}}^{t}dF\left(s\right) \tag{5.21}\]

That is, beginning from an _initial price_\(F\left(t_{0}\right)\), we obtain the price at time \(t\) by simply adding all subsequent _infinitesimal_ changes. Clearly, in continuous time, there are an uncountable number of such infinitesimal changes--hence the use of integral notation. Also, at the limit, the notation for "small" incremental changes \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) is replaced by \(dF\left(t\right)\), which represents infinitesimal changes.

Finally, consider the following question. At the limit, the infinitesimal changes \(dF\left(t\right)\) are still very erratic. Would the trajectories of \(F\left(t\right)\) be of _bounded variation_7 The question is important, because otherwise the Riemann-Stieltjes way of constructing integrals cannot be exploited and a new definition of integral would be needed.

Footnote 7: See Chapter 3.

Another important point is the following: the integral in (5.21) is taken with respect to a random process, and not with respect to a deterministic variable as is the case in standard calculus. Clearly, this integral is itself a random variable. Can such an integral be successfully defined? Can we use the Riemann-Stieltjes procedure of approximations by appropriate rectangles to construct an integral with respect to a random process? These questions lead to the Ito integral and will be answered in Chapter 9.

#### Moments

One question that we answer here concerns the moments of a binomial process.

Let \(t\) be fixed. Then the expected value and the variance of \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) are defined as follows:

\[\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta F\left(t\right)\right]=p\left(a\sqrt{\Delta}\right)+ \left(1-p\right)\left(-a\sqrt{\Delta}\right) \tag{5.22}\]

\[\mathbb{V}\left[\Delta F\left(t\right)\right] =p\left(a\sqrt{\Delta}\right)^{2}+\left(1-p\right)\left(-a\sqrt{ \Delta}\right)^{2}\] \[\quad-\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta F\left(t\right)\right]^{2} \tag{5.23}\]

If we have a 50-50 chance of an uptick at any time \(t\), then

\[p=\frac{1}{2} \tag{5.24}\]

and the expected value will equal 0 while the variance is given by \(a^{2}\Delta\).

It is important to realize that the variance of the binomial process is proportional to \(\Delta\). As \(\Delta\) approaches zero, a variance that is proportional to \(\Delta\) will go toward zero with the same speed. This means that if we think of \(\Delta\) as a small but nonnegligible quantity, then the variance will also be nonnegligible.

In contrast, if \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) had instead fluctuated between, say, \(+a\Delta\) and \(-a\Delta\) the variance would be proportional to \(\Delta^{2}\). For "small" \(\Delta\), the value of \(\Delta^{2}\) would be much smaller. When \(\Delta\to 0\), the variance would go to zero significantly faster. Under such conditions, it can be maintained without any contradiction that the variance of \(\Delta F\left(t\right)\) is negligible, while \(\Delta\) itself is not.

Heuristically speaking, a random variable with a variance proportional to \(\Delta^{2}\) will be approximately constant in infinitesimal time intervals.

Figure 5.4 illustrates the difference between a variance proportional to \(\Delta\)(the \(45^{\circ}\) line) and one proportional to \(\Delta^{2}\). The latter becomes negligible for small \(\Delta\).

This last point is also relevant for higher-order moments of the binomial process. Again assume that \(p=0.5\) for simplicity. Then the expected value is zero and the third moment will be given by

\[\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta F\left(t\right)\right]^{3}=p\left(+a\sqrt{\Delta} \right)^{3}+\left(1-p\right)\left(-a\sqrt{\Delta}\right)^{3} \tag{5.25}\]

With \(p=0.5\), the third moment equals zero.

The fourth-order moment is obtained as

\[\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta F\left(t\right)\right]^{4}=\left(+a\sqrt{\Delta} \right)^{4}=a^{4}\Delta^{2} \tag{5.26}\]

As \(\Delta\to 0\), the fourth-order moment will become negligible. It is proportional to a power of \(\Delta\) that goes to zero faster than the time interval itself.

These observations imply that for small intervals \(\Delta\), higher-order moments of a binomial random variable that assumes values proportional to can be ignored.

#### The Normal Distribution

Now consider the following experiment with the random variable \(F(t)\) discussed in the previous section. We ask the computer to calculate _many_ realizations of \(F(t)\). Then, beginning from the same initial point \(F(0)\), we plot these trajectories.

Beginning from \(t_{0}=0\), in the _immediate_ future, \(F(t)\), has only two possible values:

\[F\left(0+\Delta\right)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}F\left(0\right)+a\sqrt{ \Delta}&\text{with probability }p\\ F\left(0\right)-a\sqrt{\Delta}&\text{with probability }1-p\end{array}\right. \tag{5.27}\]

Hence, \(F\left(t\right)\) itself is binomial at \(t=0+\Delta\).

But if we let some more time pass, and then look at \(F\left(t\right)\) at, say, \(t=2\Delta,F\left(t\right)\) will assume one of three possible values. More precisely, we have the following possibilities:

\[F\left(2\Delta\right)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}F\left(0\right)+a\sqrt{ \Delta}+a\sqrt{\Delta}&\text{with probability }p^{2}\\ F\left(0\right)-a\sqrt{\Delta}+a\sqrt{\Delta}&\text{with probability }p\left(1-p\right)\\ F\left(0\right)-a\sqrt{\Delta}-a\sqrt{\Delta}&\text{with probability }\left(1-p\right)^{2} \end{array}\right. \tag{5.28}\]

That is, \(F\left(2\Delta\right)\) may equal \(F\left(0\right)+2a\sqrt{\Delta},F\left(0\right)-2a\sqrt{\Delta}\), or \(F\left(0\right)\). Of these, the last outcome is most likely if there is a 50-50 chance of an uptick.

Now consider possible values of \(F\left(t\right)\) once some more time elapses. Several more combinations of upticks and downticks become possible.

