But this is nothing other than the ordinary differential equation

\[\frac{dB\left(t,T\right)}{dt}+rB\left(t,T\right)=0\]

with terminal condition \(B\left(T\right)=1\). Its solution will be given by

\[B\left(t,T\right)=e^{-r\left(T-t\right)}\]

This bond pricing function will satisfy the boundary condition and the fundamental PDE. It is the usual discount at a constant instantaneous rate \(r\).

#### Case 2: A Mean-Reverting \(r_{t}\)

Suppose now the market price of risk is constant:

\[\lambda\left(r_{t},t\right)=\lambda \tag{20.36}\]

but that the spot rate follows the mean-reverting SDE given by:

\[dr_{t}=\alpha\left(\kappa-r_{t}\right)dt+bdW_{t} \tag{20.37}\]

where \(W_{t}\) is a Wiener process under the real-world probability. Note that the volatility structure is restricted to be a constant _absolute_ volatility denoted by \(b\). Suppose further that the parameters \(\alpha,\kappa,b\), and \(\lambda\) are known exactly. Then, the fundamental PDE for a typical \(B(t,T)\) will reduce to:

\[B_{r}\left(\alpha\left(\kappa-r_{t}\right)dt+bdW_{t}\right) \tag{20.38}\]

This setup is known as the Vasicek model, after the seminal work of Vasicek (1977).

It can be shown that the solution of this PDE is the closed-form expression given by the bond pricing formula \(B(t,T)\), for time \(t=0\),

\[B\left(0,T\right)=e^{\frac{1}{\alpha}\left(1-e^{-aT}\right)\left(R-r\right)- TR-\frac{b^{2}}{4\alpha^{3}}\left(1-e^{-aT}\right)^{2}} \tag{20.39}\]

where

\[R=\kappa-\frac{b\lambda}{\alpha}-\frac{b^{2}}{\alpha^{2}} \tag{20.40}\]

and \(r\) is the current observation on the spot rate. Given some plausible estimates for the unknown parameters, we can then plot this function.

#### Example

For example, consider an economy where the long-run mean of the spot rate is \(5\%\) and where the spot rate is pulled toward the long-run mean at a rate of \(0.25\). We thus have

\[\alpha=0.25\quad\kappa=0.05 \tag{20.41}\]

Further, suppose the absolute interest rate volatility is \(.015\) during one year:

\[b=0.015 \tag{20.42}\]

To apply the formula, we need the market price of interest rate risk. Assume that we have

\[\mu-r_{t}=-0.1\sigma \tag{20.43}\]

where the \(\mu,\sigma\) are the unknown bond drift and volatility parameters. Then, we know that

\[\lambda=-0.10 \tag{20.44}\]

Using these parameters, we can calculate the bond pricing function \(B(t,T)\) that will depend on the initial interest rate \(r\) and on the maturity parameter \(T\). This is the so-called "discount curve"discussed earlier.

The graph of the \(\left\{B(t,T),T\in\left[0,T_{\max}\right]\right\}\) with \(\left\{\lambda=-0.10,b=0.015,\alpha=0.25,\kappa=0.05\right\}\) at three different levels for the spot rate \(r=0.5\%,r=5\%,r=15\%\) are shown in Figure 20.1. Because these are discount bond prices, the short maturities have values close to \(1\), whereas longer maturities get progressively cheaper.

The corresponding _yield curve_ is obtained by taking (minus) the logarithm of the discount curve and then dividing by the maturity. The yield curve is shown in Figure 20.2 for the same set of initial spot rates.

Note that the mean-reverting aspect of the interest rate SDE determines that the yield curve can have upward- or downward-sloping curves, as well as flat ones. This is because if the spot rate is 15% currently, the model assumes that it will go back toward its mean 5% as we consider the long bonds. Thus long bonds would automatically be priced by using rates on the average around 5%, whereas short bonds will be priced by using short rates closer to 15%. The case of a current short rate below the long-run mean is the reverse and gives an upward-sloping yield curve.

Figure 20.3 shows the effect of changing the value of market price of risk \(\lambda\) on the discount curve, assuming \(r=5\%\).

