Many derivative instruments, including vanilla options, caps, floors, and swaptions, can be expressed as a simple call or put option. For that reason, this setup can be used to price those instruments.

The FFT approach can be used for any model for which a characteristic function for the asset price distribution exists. It can be applied for strictly path independent European options and for a restricted set of terminal payoffs. The method is fast and for \(n\) option prices is \(O(n\log(n))\) time and generates \(n\) option prices in a single run. The problem with the scheme is that it needs to be re-derived for any change in the payoff structure. It requires estimation of proper \(\alpha\) and can be exceedingly inaccurate for highly out of the money options.

